D.C. council committee advances online casino bill

The Washington, D.C. Council's Human Services Committee voted 3-1 on November 18 to advance a bill legalizing online casinos. It sets no cap on licences, a $2 million application fee and a 25% tax on gross gaming revenue, estimated at about $50 million a year. The bill bans credit card deposits, sets the minimum age at 21 and requires interventions for heavy-depositing players under 25. Supporters say residents already wager about $1 billion offshore; opponent Christina Henderson argues the safeguards are not enough. The full Council still has to vote, and a 2027 launch is possible.

